## Decision

Freeze the first citable baseline only after tasks 1 through 5 are implemented. A baseline is an immutable, versioned corpus result, not a mutable latest file.

Commit machine-readable JSON and a human-readable Markdown rendering under a baseline series identified by the evaluation-contract version. Each artifact records:

- scanner commit;
- corpus/gold digest;
- evaluation-contract version;
- taxonomy and adapter-map digests;
- dependency lock digest;
- every materialized source-repository commit;
- accepted, proposed, ambiguous, and rejected counts plus review provenance;
- deterministic configuration, including AI disabled.

Required packet-and-layer fields include evaluable positives, matched positives, exact-concept recall, ancestor-category recall where applicable, label/classification accuracy, strict correct-label recall, precision, unread count/rate, distinct evidence-file coverage, ineligible reasons, unscorable findings, and gate exceptions. Totals are denominator-weighted only within the same layer. No scalar overall mixes heterogeneous layers.

The official run uses scanner `develop`, pinned materializations verified against manifests, accepted gold only, and the canonical evaluator. A language/capability slice accompanies the full report so unsupported-language coverage cannot hide results on supported inputs.

Detector changes are compared against a frozen baseline and do not overwrite it. Gold or contract changes start a new immutable series. For every contract change, run the old and new contracts against the same scanner commit once as a bridge so the metric discontinuity is measured.

CI always validates schema, contract fixtures, digests, and deterministic rendering. Full materialized-corpus execution may be scheduled, manual, or release-gated if cloning all upstream repositories is unsuitable for each pull request; a representative pinned smoke subset can run on pull requests. Any published full baseline must include authenticated materialization validation.

Rejected alternatives:

- averaging packet percentages;
- a cross-layer overall score;
- overwriting the prior baseline after detector changes;
- unpinned or AI-assisted headline runs;
- CLI-copy or Plexus results before they use the canonical evaluator;
- recall published without eligibility and provenance.

The old mixed CLI run and any pre-contract scanner run remain historical diagnostics, not this baseline.
